Chapter: LeafMispredict

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Regression: leaf-profile sizes a minimal leaf frame before the body is
 emitted, so it predicts the emitter's temp use and can guess low. Both
 shapes below profile at exactly four locals and then need a fifth --
 m-swap's tail arguments permute and need a staging temp, p-shift's tail
 argument nests and needs more than one temp per complex argument. Each
 overran the frame it had been promised, and the emitter rejected valid
 Codex with CDX2000 rather than emitting it.

 A mispredict is now a retry through the standard path, so what these
 pin is that both compile at all and that the fallback emits working
 code: m-swap 7 111 222 = 222 (the arguments really do swap each
 iteration), p-shift 5 3 = -33.

Section: Definitions

  m-swap : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  m-swap (n) (a) (b) = if n == 0 then a else m-swap (n - 1) b a

  p-shift : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  p-shift (n) (a) = if n == 0 then a else p-shift (n - 1) (a + a - n)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show (m-swap 7 111 222))
    print-line-uni (show (p-shift 5 3))
  end

